            "title": "10.2% Yield Dives Into Our Strong Buy Range From ARMOUR Residential REIT",
            "description": "<html><body><p data-eci=\"true\"><figure class=\"getty-figure\" data-type=\"getty-image\"><picture> <img alt=\"Portrait Photo Of Cute Dog Outdoors Looking at the Camera\" data-id=\"2268945105\" data-type=\"getty-image\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"1024\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 132px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 666px), (max-width: 1307px) calc(100vw - 708px), 600px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w630\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w1536 1536w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w1280 1280w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w1080 1080w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w750 750w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w640 640w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w480 480w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w320 320w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2268945105/image_2268945105.jpg?io=getty-c-w240 240w\" width=\"1536\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"> </p> <p class=\"item-credits\">kankarash_tara/E+ via Getty Images</p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>Sometimes preferred shares dip into our buy range.</p> <p>Other times, they dive-bomb it.</p> <p>ARMOUR Residential REIT’s (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/ARR\" title=\"ARMOUR Residential REIT, Inc.\">ARR</a>) preferred share ARR-C (<span class=\"ticker-hover-wrapper\"><a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/ARR.PR.C\" title=\"ARMOUR Residential REIT, Inc. 7% SER C CUM PFD\">ARR.PR.C</a></span>) fell around 9% in a month. Most of that happened in about a week. If you owned the shares, the following chart is a bit obnoxious.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"479\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"821\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"373\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075965_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>Seeking Alpha</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>The market took roughly a year’s worth of dividends out of the share price. ARMOUR is still paying the same dividend.</p> <p>ARR-C was relatively quiet until around November 9. Over the last week, the 10-year Treasury yield went from 4.88% to over 5.10%.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"724\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1327\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"349\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837891159706_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>FRED</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>When investors can earn more from Treasuries, they will generally start asking for more yield on ARR-C.</p> <p>If short-term rates fall, a floating-rate preferred can start sending smaller dividends. ARR-C keeps sending the same $1.75 annual dividend. If rates go up, we get the same $1.75 dividend while new buyers can get the same dividend for a lower price.</p> <h2>ARR-C In Strong Buy Range</h2> <p>ARR-C recently traded around $17.12. That puts shares $1.55 below our buy rating. It’s below our strong buy range by a whopping penny as shown in this chart from <a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/mp/1060-the-reit-forum/articles\">The REIT Forum</a>.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"121\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"false\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"false\" data-og-image-msn=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"false\" data-width=\"1067\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"73\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-1795183789075771_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>The REIT Forum</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <ul> <li><p>Strong buy under: $17.13</p></li> <li><p>Buy under: $18.67</p></li> <li><p>Overpriced: $20.34</p></li> </ul> <p>At $17.12, ARR-C offers a stripped yield of 10.24% and carries a risk rating of 2 on our scale from 1 to 5.</p> <p><a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4724655-7-72-percent-preferred-share-from-armour-residential\">In our 2024 article</a>, ARR-C was yielding 7.72%. At that time, we thought investors had better opportunities elsewhere. Today, we like the price a lot more.</p> <p>You might’ve noticed that our valuation table shows the annualized yield to call as “Absurd.” ARMOUR can call ARR-C at $25. That option has been available since January 2025. A call at $25 would give investors a gain of $7.88 per share at recent prices and before dividends. ARMOUR would have to volunteer to pay that.</p> <p>We’re going to go ahead and base our analysis on the 10.24% stripped yield.</p> <h2>ARR-C Dividend</h2> <p>ARR-C pays a monthly dividend which may be attractive to investors. Most preferred shares we cover pay quarterly. The monthly dividend is convenient if investors are using it to cover monthly expenses. Your bills don’t consider dividend payment schedules.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"121\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"false\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"false\" data-og-image-msn=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"false\" data-width=\"1188\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"65\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/11/20/14527192-17951837890708494_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>The REIT Forum</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>At the time of posting this chart, there was about two cents towards the next dividend. There has been very little dividend accrual so this only takes the stripped yield from 10.22% to 10.24%.</p> <p>The common equity to preferred liquidation ratio for ARR preferred shares is 11.91. That’s a lot of common equity to cover the preferred shares. That’s generally something we want to see for our preferred shares that come with a lower risk rating. In general, common equity would take the losses first in a worst-case scenario. This is a substantial cushion for preferred shareholders and one of the reasons we assigned a risk rating of 2.</p> <h3>The Common Shares</h3> <p>There are a few issues investors should know about. Despite the low risk rating, ARR-C does face a few unique challenges. Obviously, it is a fixed-rate share. That increases the exposure to fluctuations in value from interest rates.</p> <p>The other issue is that the common shares have underperformed frequently over the last decade. I’ve been a major critic of the REIT for delivering unimpressive total economic returns (change in book value + dividend) over the last 10+ years. That doesn’t mean there were no positive periods, but overall it was pretty unimpressive.</p> <p>I believe that poor decisions in portfolio management remain a risk, though the agency MBS structure of the portfolio significantly reduces that risk. They don’t have to be significantly more dangerous than the other agency mortgage REITs. However, historically, they have underperformed AGNC Investment (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/AGNC\" title=\"AGNC Investment Corp.\">AGNC</a>), Annaly Capital (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/NLY\" title=\"Annaly Capital Management, Inc.\">NLY</a>), and Dynex Capital (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/DX\" title=\"Dynex Capital, Inc.\">DX</a>) by a substantial amount. Consequently, I would warn investors that the common tends to decline more than those peers.</p> <p>That hasn’t ruined the coverage ratio for the preferred equity because the REIT continued to issue enough common shares to maintain a solid coverage ratio. Because AGNC trades at a substantially higher price-to-book, I would love to see AGNC try to acquire ARR. If such an acquisition happened, it would be interesting for ARR-C. AGNC has weaker coverage ratios for preferred shares due to more preferred equity outstanding, but they have done a better job managing the REIT.</p> <p>The best case scenario would be an acquisition by DX, but the price-to-book for DX is lower. To make an acquisition make sense for the common shareholders, a large disparity in price-to-book is very helpful. Finally, I should mention that there have been many instances where ARR trades at a materially lower price-to-book than those peers and there hasn’t been an acquisition. Consequently, I am not forecasting one. I am merely saying that if one occurred, I think the market would treat it favorably for the preferred shares. Each of those other mortgage REITs has one series of fixed-rate preferred shares and each of them trades at a lower yield than ARR-C despite having a higher price (less upside). Consequently, if ARR were acquired by one of those REITs, I think ARR-C would do quite well.</p> <h2>Final Thoughts</h2> <p>At $17.12, ARR-C is just barely in our strong buy range. Even if prices go up a few pennies, shares are still well within our normal buy range.</p> <p>We like the risk/reward here of a 10.24% stripped yield and the potential for shares to sell off again if rates rise. On the flip side, there is a lot of room for price recovery if interest rates start dropping.</p></body></html>",
